  "enhanced_information": "The case presents a 16-year-old woman with primary amenorrhea, normal secondary sexual characteristics, and normal estradiol and testosterone levels. The gynecological examination reveals vaginal agenesis and ultrasound shows absence of the uterus, while the ovaries are normal. The absence of the left kidney suggests a developmental anomaly. This clinical presentation is consistent with Rokitansky syndrome (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome), characterized by congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, often associated with renal anomalies. The reasoning chains correctly identify Rokitansky syndrome as the most likely diagnosis, supported by the absence of androgen excess, which rules out congenital adrenal hyperplasia and polycystic ovarian syndrome. Morris syndrome is also ruled out due to the presence of normal secondary sexual characteristics. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is Rokitansky syndrome.",
  "analysis": "The clinical presentation of the 16-year-old woman includes primary amenorrhea, normal secondary sexual characteristics, normal estradiol and testosterone levels, vaginal agenesis, absence of the uterus, and normal ovaries. These findings are indicative of Rokitansky syndrome (Mayer-Rokitansky-Küster-Hauser syndrome), which is characterized by congenital absence of the uterus and vagina, often associated with renal anomalies such as the absence of a kidney. The normal levels of estradiol and testosterone help rule out conditions like Morris syndrome, which would typically present with abnormal secondary sexual characteristics due to androgen insensitivity. Additionally, congenital adrenal hyperplasia and polycystic ovarian syndrome are ruled out due to the absence of androgen excess and the presence of normal ovarian structure. Kallman syndrome is also unlikely as it typically presents with hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and anosmia, which are not indicated in this case. Therefore, the most likely diagnosis is Rokitansky syndrome, supported by the combination of findings. The reasoning aligns with established medical consensus on the condition.",
